---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-new-zealand`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in New Zealand',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin D deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in New Zealand', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in New Zealand: Sun Safety, Latitude, and Winter Dosing"
  description="New Zealand spans 34–47°S with intense summer UV but a real southern winter. How the Ministry of Health balances skin-cancer risk against vitamin D, and who needs a supplement."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-new-zealand", label: "Vitamin D in New Zealand" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in New Zealand</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      New Zealand faces the same balancing act as Australia, sharpened by geography. It runs from about
      34°S in the far north to 47°S at the bottom of the South Island, so the north is nearly
      subtropical while the south has a proper cool-temperate winter. Add some of the clearest air and
      highest summer UV levels on Earth, and you get a country that has to warn people <em>away</em> from
      the sun in summer and worry about vitamin D in winter — often for the same person.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The sun-safety tension</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      New Zealand and Australia have among the highest melanoma rates in the world, so the public-health
      message in summer is firmly protective — hat, shade, sunscreen, especially between 10am and 4pm.
      The official position, shared with Australian bodies, is that in <strong>summer</strong> most
      people make plenty of vitamin D incidentally and do not need deliberate sun exposure. In
      <strong>winter</strong>, particularly in the lower South Island, the calculus flips: a bit of
      midday sun on exposed skin becomes helpful, and some people need a supplement.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How common is deficiency?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      National survey data show that a meaningful minority of New Zealanders have low vitamin D, with
      levels dropping in winter and spring and lowest in the south. Rates are higher among Māori,
      Pacific, and South Asian populations with darker skin, in older adults, and in those who avoid the
      sun. As with Australia, "sunny country" does not mean "vitamin D replete" once behaviour and
      season are taken into account.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Guidance and dosing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The Ministry of Health does not recommend routine supplementation for the general population, but
      targets at-risk groups: people with naturally very dark skin, those who are housebound or cover
      up, and certain older adults, for whom a supplement (commonly a monthly higher-dose tablet in
      practice, or a daily dose) is used. Fortification is limited, so diet contributes little. See our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-in-australia" class="text-amber-700 underline">Australia</a> and
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-sun-safety" class="text-amber-700 underline">sun-safety</a> pages for the
      shared reasoning.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>In summer: sun-protect — you will make enough vitamin D incidentally.</li>
      <li>In winter, especially in the South Island: a little midday sun helps, and at-risk people should consider a supplement.</li>
      <li>Darker-skinned, housebound, and older New Zealanders are the priority groups for testing or supplementation.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> New Zealand's UV can cause skin damage in minutes in summer —
      never trade sun safety for vitamin D. If you think you are at risk of deficiency, ask your GP
      rather than sunbathing.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-new-zealand" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
